pair A, B, N;

A = (1,1);
B = (-1/2,3/2);
N = intersectionpoint(A + (-2,-2)--(1,1), x^2 - x/2 + 3/2);

real parab (real x) {
  return(x^2);
}

draw(graph(parab,-2,2));
draw((1,-4)--(A + (1,2)));
draw((1,2)--(A + (-2,4)));
draw(A--B--N--cycle);

dot("$A$", A, S);
dot("$B$", B, SW);
dot("$N$", N, NE);
[/asy]

Note: The intersection $B$ of the normal line with the parabola is found by solving 
\[-\dfrac{1}{2}x + \dfrac{3}{2} = x^2.\] This gives $2x^2 + x - 3 = 0,$ which factors as $(2x - 3)(x + 1) = 0.$ So $x = -\dfrac{1}{2}$ or $x = 1.$ We discard $x = 1,$ since it corresponds to the point $A,$ and we accept $x = -\dfrac{1}{2},$ giving the point $B = \left(-\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{9}{4}\right).$</think>
Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{\left(-\dfrac{1}{2}, \dfrac{9}{4}\right)}.$

